  "text": "Question three. What is V lookup, and what are its limits? V lookup, short for vertical lookup, finds a key in the first column of a range and returns a value from a column to its right, by number. P one oh two, column three: nine dollars. It only looks right. Ask it for the mug's stock-keeping unit, its S K U, and it searches the S K U column for the word mug, and comes back N A: not available. And that column number is typed in. Insert a category column, and column three is now the category: the mug's price comes back as kitchen. Question four. How does index match work, and why is it more robust? Match finds the position of a value in one range. Index returns what sits at that position in another. So it can look left: find mug among the names, and return its S K U. And it points at ranges, not a column number, so after the insert the price range moves with the data, and P one oh two still costs nine. Question five. What does X lookup improve over both? It's the newer lookup, in current versions of Excel. A lookup array and a return array, so any direction, in one function. Exact match by default. And a built-in if not found: P one oh nine says not found, with no if error around it. Which brings up the argument everyone forgets.",
